WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.630
What happens when a quadratic has no real
roots? You can use the quadratic formula.

00:04.630 --> 00:09.260
In this lesson we solve quadratic
equations that have complex roots. By the

00:09.260 --> 00:13.891
end, Solve quadratics with a negative
discriminant.

00:13.891 --> 00:17.455
The quadratic formula still applies. The
only new step is taking the square root of

00:17.455 --> 00:21.019
a negative discriminant.

00:21.019 --> 00:25.233
In x squared plus six x plus thirteen
equals zero, the discriminant is thirty

00:25.233 --> 00:29.448
six minus fifty two, which is minus
sixteen.

00:29.448 --> 00:34.377
The square root of minus sixteen is four
i. Dividing by two gives the conjugate

00:34.377 --> 00:39.307
pair minus three plus two i and minus
three minus two i.

00:39.307 --> 00:44.728
On the Argand diagram the two roots are
mirror images across the real axis.

00:44.728 --> 00:48.747
Your turn. Solve x squared plus two x plus
five equals zero. Pause the video, then

00:48.747 --> 00:52.765
check: the discriminant is minus sixteen,
so the roots are minus one plus or minus

00:52.765 --> 00:56.784
two i.

00:56.784 --> 01:00.340
A common mistake is to stop when the
discriminant is negative. Pause and decide

01:00.340 --> 01:03.897
whether that is right. A negative
discriminant gives a complex conjugate

01:03.897 --> 01:07.453
pair.

01:07.453 --> 01:11.913
Your turn. Solve x squared plus two x plus
five equals zero. Pause the video. Hint:

01:11.913 --> 01:16.373
The discriminant is minus sixteen.

01:16.373 --> 01:22.348
The roots are minus one plus or minus two
i. In a new context, x squared plus nine

01:22.348 --> 01:28.323
equals zero has roots plus or minus three
i. Rate your confidence from one to five.

01:28.323 --> 01:33.177
A negative discriminant gives a complex
conjugate pair. The formula does the rest.

01:33.177 --> 01:38.032
Next, we use conjugate roots to rebuild
polynomials.
